; Cranelisp — modules and imports
; The standard library is modules. You pull names in on demand.

; /imports shows what is currently in scope. At the start that is just the
; prelude (operators, the common types, Some/None) plus special forms:

/imports

; The collection verbs are NOT bare — they live in collections.vec. Import
; the ones you want by name; the REPL confirms each:

(import [collections.vec [count get assoc]])
/imports

; An imported name reports its fully-qualified origin — /info shows its
; signature, docstring, and source, and typing it bare gives the one-line
; description:

/info count
count

; Now use them unqualified:

(count (vec 10 20 30))
(get (vec 10 20 30) 1)
(assoc (vec 10 20 30) 0 99)

; List operations live in collections.list — first and rest return Option;
; match takes the tail apart:

(import [collections.list [first rest]])
(first (list 1 2 3))
(match (rest (list 1 2 3)) [(Some more) (first more) None None])

; A name you have not imported is simply unbound — the REPL tells you what
; it knows, it does not guess. Type one and see:

vec-reverse

; Import it from its module and it resolves:

(import [collections.vec [vec-reverse]])
(vec-reverse (vec 1 2 3))

; You can import just a trait's METHOD — you do not need the trait itself in
; scope. Dispatch follows from the method's own identity. `default` lives in
; the `default` module; import the method alone:

(import [default [default]])

; /imports now lists `default` under Fns — the `Default` trait name is NOT in
; scope (it is not among the prelude names), yet the method dispatches:

/imports
default

; `default` yields a type's zero value, chosen by the RETURN type. Annotate the
; type you want and dispatch resolves to that impl — no trait import needed:

:Int (default)
:String (default)
:Bool (default)

; Call it bare and nothing pins the return type, so inference cannot choose an
; impl. The REPL says exactly what to do — an actionable error, never an opaque
; failure:

(default)

; This is how the Sudoku exemplar is built: grid, solver, html, and form
; are four modules, each importing only what it needs from the others and
; from the prelude. `cranelisp exemplar/user.cl` wires them together.
